Namespace: In Kubernetes, namespaces provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources within a single cluster.
A Namespace in Kubernetes is a logical partition inside a cluster.

$ kubectcl get namespaces
$ kubectl create namespace app-a
$ vi mypod.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
	name: nginx-pod
	namespace: app-a
spec:
	containers:
	-  name: nginx-container
	   image: nginx
	   ports:
	     - containerPort: 80

$ kubectl apply of mypod.yaml
$ kubectl get pod
$ kubectl get pod -n app-a




Service account:
In K8s there are two types of identities: Humans (authenticated externally like IAM,OIDC,Certificate,..), application running inside pod
Pod also needs an identity to run
$ kubectl get sa

A service account is a Kubernetes object that provides an identity to PODS.
Since pods also sends API requests to api server to needs some identity to communicate
Authentication to Kubernetes APT
Authorization via RBAC
Secure communication between pod and cluster
Every namespace automatically gets a serviceaccount named default

$ kubectl create serviceaccount app-sa -n app-a
$ kubectl get sa -n app-a
$ vi mypo.yaml 

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
	name: test-pod
	namespace: app-a
spec:
	serviceAccountName: app-sa
	containers:
	-  name: nginx
	   image: nginx
	   
$ kubectl apply -f mypod.yaml
$ kubectl get po -n app-a
$ kubectl describe po test-pod -n app-a


Roles & RoleBindings

Lets say we have a pod which is running inside a custom namespace and a custom sa is attached to it 
Roles are created for namespace and restrict it via some permissions which is rolebindings
Role defines a set of permission inside a Namespace like which API group u have access, which resource, what are the actions allowed and we attach this role to a service account known as rolebinding


